<h2>The challenge</h2><p>In retail, forecast quality affects decisions about stock, operations, and planning. A single algorithm may not perform well across every demand pattern. Evaluation must also respect time ordering: random splits can create an unrealistically optimistic view of accuracy.</p>
<h2>Technical approach</h2><ul><li>Establish interpretable statistical baselines, including classical time-series methods and intermittent-demand approaches.</li><li>Compare global ML methods, including gradient-boosted trees, with local statistical models and ensemble strategies.</li><li>Use rolling or backtesting windows and evaluate errors using business-relevant forecasting metrics such as WMAPE.</li><li>Study performance by demand pattern, rather than relying solely on a single global score.</li></ul>
<h2>The judgment behind the work</h2><p>The key scientific decision is not simply which model wins a leaderboard. It is how to choose a model family under different data conditions, control temporal leakage, and balance complexity against maintainability.</p>
<div class="callout">Why this project is here: it illustrates a combination of modeling judgment, engineering tradeoffs, and attention to the decisions the system supports—not a claim of performance without evidence.</div>
<h2>Evidence to add</h2><p>A future revision can include approved baseline comparisons, error distributions, series-segment breakdowns, and a reproducible synthetic demonstration.</p>
